&lt;p&gt;Former Program Director, NCI/NIH&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Title: Curating and Administering a Portfolio at the Intersection of Iron and Cancer&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Abstract:&amp;nbsp;As the role of iron metabolism in cancer progression and therapy gains prominence, establishing a cohesive research landscape requires a blend of scientific insight and administrative experience. Drawing on my experience as Program Director, this presentation provides a dual-perspective look at building a high-impact research portfolio from the ground up. I will first detail the strategic development of the &amp;quot;Iron and Cancer&amp;quot; portfolio, highlighting how emerging trends like ferroptosis and dysregulation of iron metabolism in cancer were identified and prioritized. The discussion will then shift to the mechanics of program administration. I will outline the lifecycle of grants, portfolio management&amp;mdash;from initial curation and funding oversight to the long-term stewardship of funded projects. Attendees will gain a transparent understanding of how programmatic leadership transforms a niche scientific interest into a robust, sustainable research program.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Host: Brent Stockwell&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Lunchtime with IRCPL&lt;br /&gt;
&lt;a href="https://www.ircpl.columbia.edu/calendar/getting-loud-at-the-library-interfaith-dialogue-in-old-books" target="_blank"&gt;Getting Loud at the Library: Interfaith Dialogue in Old Books&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Date: &lt;/strong&gt;Monday, October 5, 2026 from 12:00-2:00PM&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Location:&lt;/strong&gt; The Burke Library (conference room) at Union Theological Seminary, 3rd Floor&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Series:&lt;/strong&gt; Lunchtime with IRCPL&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Who says you can&amp;#39;t talk in the library? Just as you gather in Butler or Burke Library with friends and acquaintances to talk about books, debate ideas, work on your research, and even just hang out, so too did bookish people of the past.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The library has always been a meeting place for people from distinct religious communities. They often spoke different languages, and they generally disagreed about many things. But they engaged in dialogue. How, exactly? And why?&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Come join IRCPL Director Seth Kimmel and Burke Library&amp;#39;s Collection Services Librarian Jeffrey Wayno to explore some rare books and manuscripts that tell the stories of these encounters, and stay for the free lunch!&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Lunch is provided,&lt;/strong&gt; and all are welcome.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://docs.google.com/forms/d/e/1FAIpQLScVvgP1zt383KNjkBPjUT-iJkdMw6wYA6oSEpm9O_LM7nZPnA/viewform" target="_blank"&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Register here&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;If you do not have a Columbia University ID, pre-registration is required by 11:59 on Sunday, October 4 for campus access.&lt;/p&gt;

