&lt;p&gt;ABOUT THE EVENT&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Starting from the recent UNESCO recognition of Italian cooking as an intangible cultural heritage of humanity, the conversation will explore how food culture can become a driver of sustainability, circular economy, and climate responsibility.&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Food is one of the most powerful lenses through which to understand the climate crisis: it connects land, water, energy, agriculture, transport, consumption, waste and social justice.&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;In a world marked by global warming, geopolitical instability and growing food insecurity, preventing food waste is not only an environmental priority, but also a concrete way to reduce emissions, protect resources and build more resilient food systems.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;This event marks the 10th anniversary of the Italian Academy&amp;rsquo;s &lt;/em&gt;&lt;a href="https://italianacademy.columbia.edu/content/international-observatory-cultural-heritage"&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;strong&gt;International Observatory for Cultural Heritage&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;em&gt;.&amp;nbsp;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
