&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;Chile, Obstinate Memory&lt;/em&gt; (1997) and Post-Screening Conversation&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;&amp;ldquo;A country without documentary cinema is like a family without a photo album.&amp;quot; &amp;mdash;Patricio Guzm&amp;aacute;n&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Due to the passing of Chilean exile director &lt;strong&gt;Patricio Guzm&amp;aacute;n&lt;/strong&gt; Tuesday, September 15, in Paris, Columbia University will be honoring the monumental legacy of one of the world&amp;rsquo;s foremost documentary filmmakers. For the occasion, we are pleased to be screening his landmark film &lt;em&gt;Chile, Obstinate Memory&lt;/em&gt; (1997) on a 35mm print.&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The screening will be followed by an all women-panel of Chilean artists, filmmakers, and scholars who will talk about Guzm&amp;aacute;n&amp;rsquo;s legacy from a plurality of approaches and feminist perspectives. The conversation will be moderated by Associate Professor &lt;strong&gt;Elizabeth Ram&amp;iacute;rez-Soto&lt;/strong&gt; (Film and Media Studies, Columbia University School of the Arts), and guests include &lt;strong&gt;Cecilia Vicu&amp;ntilde;a &lt;/strong&gt;(artist), &lt;strong&gt;Daffne Vald&amp;eacute;s Vargas&lt;/strong&gt; (Colectivo Lastesis), &lt;strong&gt;Nicole Costa&lt;/strong&gt; (director of &lt;em&gt;The Journey of Mona Lisa&lt;/em&gt;), Associate Professor &lt;strong&gt;Mar&amp;iacute;a Jos&amp;eacute; Contreras&lt;/strong&gt; (Theatre, Columbia University School of the Arts), and more to be confirmed, so stay tuned!&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;This event is organized by the School of the Arts Film and Media Studies Program.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Co-Sponsors&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;br /&gt;
Department of History&lt;br /&gt;
Center for the Study of Ethnicity and Race (CSER)&lt;br /&gt;
Institute of Latin American Studies (ILAS)&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;IIJS is pleased to welcome &lt;strong&gt;Prof. Nicholas Lemann&lt;/strong&gt; for a discussion of his new book, &lt;em&gt;Returning: A Search for Home Across Three Centuries&lt;/em&gt;, joined by IIJS &lt;strong&gt;Prof. Jeremy Dauber&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;br /&gt;
&lt;br /&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Tuesday, October 13, at noon | 617 Kent Hall&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Nicholas Lemann&lt;/strong&gt;, a veteran New Yorker correspondent, grew up in New Orleans, the son of German Jews in a world of gilded privilege. Yet in contrast to his parents&amp;rsquo; generation, which always sought to downplay their religious background, Lemann was intrigued by his roots, thinking he wanted to be like Jack Burden, the ever-curious reporter in Robert Penn Warren&amp;rsquo;s All the King&amp;rsquo;s Men.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;And like his fictional hero, who gets drawn into a web of Southern political intrigue, Lemann in Returning delves deeply into the family story&amp;mdash;from their arrival in the 1830s as peddlers from Germany, to their becoming plantation owners and department store owners after the Civil War, to their emergence as aspirants in the aristocratic world of New Orleans, where they could never quite belong.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Nicholas Lemann&lt;/strong&gt; was born and raised in New Orleans. He began his journalism career there as a 17-year-old staff writer for an alternate weekly paper called the Vieux Carre Courier. He graduated from Harvard College, where he was president of The Harvard Crimson, in 1976, magna cum laude in American History and Literature. He has worked as a reporter and editor at The Washington Monthly, Texas Monthly, The Washington Post, The Atlantic (where he was national correspondent from 1983 to 1999) and The New Yorker (where he has been a staff writer for twenty-five years), and contributed to many other publications. From 2003 to 2013 he was dean of Columbia Journalism School, leading a period of significant growth and change for the school, and since then he has been a professor there. At Columbia he has also helped launch Columbia Global Reports, a publishing venture that he continues to lead, Columbia World Projects, and the Knight Columbia First Amendment Institute. He is currently one of three co-chairs of the university&amp;rsquo;s antisemitism task force. His books include The Promised Land (1991), The Big Test (1999), Redemption (2006), Transaction Man (2019), and, most recently, Higher Admissions (2024). He is a member of several honorary societies, including the American Philosophical Society, the New York Institute for the Humanities, the Society of American Historians, the American Academy of Political and Social Science, and the American Academy of Arts and Sciences, where he serves as co-chair of the academy&amp;rsquo;s Commission on Reimagining Our Economy.&lt;/p&gt;
